 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "Wl2mxM4JBQAJ",
   "from": "Stéphane Laurent",
   "author": "129e07bdb789652f",
   "date": "2016-03-06T17:01:37Z",
   "subject": "LKJ prior - Sigma not positive definite or not symmetric",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hello, <div><br></div><div>This is my first attempt to fit a multivariate normal distribution with (r)stan using a LKJ prior. My code is given below. I set a zero mean so that the covariance matrix is the only unknown parameter.</div><div><br></div><div>I get some numerical problems : <br><div><br></div></div><blockquote style=\"margin:0 0 0 40px;border:none;padding:0px\"><div><div><i>validate transformed params: Sigma is not positive definite</i></div></div><div><div><i>validate transformed params: Sigma is not symmetric. Sigma[1,2] = -nan, but Sigma[2,1] = -nan </i></div></div><div><div><i>Exception thrown at line 23: lkj_corr_log: y is not positive definite</i></div></div><div><div><i>validate transformed params: Sigma is not symmetric. Sigma[1,2] = inf, but Sigma[2,1] = inf</i></div></div></blockquote><div><div><br></div><div>Is it important ? Is there a way to avoid these problems ?</div><div>This apart, the results look quite good.</div><div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">library</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rstan</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>rstan_options</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">auto_write </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>options</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">mc</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">cores </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> parallel</span><span style=\"color:#660\">::</span><span style=\"color:#000\">detectCores</span><span style=\"color:#660\">())</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br>stancode </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;data {<br>  int&lt;lower=1&gt; N; // number of observations<br>  int&lt;lower=1&gt; J; // dimension of observations<br>  vector[J] y[N]; // observations<br>  vector[J] zero; // a vector of zeros (means of observations)<br>  int&lt;lower=1&gt; nu; // shape of the LKJ prior<br>}<br>parameters {<br>  corr_matrix[J] Omega; <br>  vector&lt;lower=0&gt;[J] sigma; <br>}<br>transformed parameters {<br>  cov_matrix[J] Sigma; // varcov matrix <br>  for(i in 1:J){<br>    for(j in 1:J){<br>      Sigma[i,j] &lt;- sigma[i] * sigma[j] * Omega[i,j];<br>    }<br>  }<br>}<br>model {<br>  y ~ multi_normal(zero,Sigma);<br>  sigma ~ gamma(0.1, 0.1);<br>  Omega ~ lkj_corr(nu);<br>}&#39;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br></span><span style=\"color:#800\"># compile model  </span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>stanmodel </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> stan_model</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">model_code </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> stancode</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> model_name</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;stanmodel_multinormal&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br><br></span><span style=\"color:#800\"># data </span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#606\">Omega</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> rbind</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>sigma </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#606\">Sigma</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> diag</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">sigma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">%*%</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Omega</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">%*%</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> diag</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">sigma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>N </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">100</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>y </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mvtnorm</span><span style=\"color:#660\">::</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rmvnorm</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">N</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Sigma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br></span><span style=\"color:#800\"># stan data </span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>standata </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> list</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">J </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> N</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">N</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> y </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> zero</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rep</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> nu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br></span><span style=\"color:#800\"># stan inits </span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>inits </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">function</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">chain_id</span><span style=\"color:#660\">){</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  L </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> list</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#606\">Omega</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#606\">Omega</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sigma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">sigma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rgamma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">10</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">10</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">return</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">L</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br><br></span><span style=\"color:#800\"># run stan</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>samples </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sampling</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">stanmodel</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> data </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> standata</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> init</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">inits</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> <br>                    iter </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">10000</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> warmup </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1000</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> chains </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">4</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br></span></div></code></div><div><br><br></div></div></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "u2XOVpINBQAJ",
   "from": "Ben Goodrich",
   "author": "743bbacc59f4b25f",
   "date": "2016-03-06T18:10:35Z",
   "subject": "Re: LKJ prior - Sigma not positive definite or not symmetric",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">On Sunday, March 6, 2016 at 12:01:37 PM UTC-5, Stéphane Laurent wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Hello, <div><br></div><div>This\r\n is my first attempt to fit a multivariate normal distribution with \r\n(r)stan using a LKJ prior. My code is given below. I set a zero mean so \r\nthat the covariance matrix is the only unknown parameter.</div><div><br></div><div>I get some numerical problems : <br><div><br></div></div><blockquote style=\"margin:0 0 0 40px;border:none;padding:0px\"><div><div><i>validate transformed params: Sigma is not positive definite</i></div></div><div><div><i>validate transformed params: Sigma is not symmetric. Sigma[1,2] = -nan, but Sigma[2,1] = -nan </i></div></div><div><div><i>Exception thrown at line 23: lkj_corr_log: y is not positive definite</i></div></div><div><div><i>validate transformed params: Sigma is not symmetric. Sigma[1,2] = inf, but Sigma[2,1] = inf</i></div></div></blockquote><div><div><br></div><div>Is it important ? Is there a way to avoid these problems ?</div><div>This apart, the results look quite good.</div></div></div></blockquote><br></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">A couple numerical failures is not that bad and fixing them won&#39;t change your results. But you can<br><ol><li>Declare Sigma as a matrix rather than a cov_matrix, in which case it will not check that it is symmetric and positive definite. This should only be done when the expression for Sigma is positive definite by construction, which it is in your case.</li><li>Better, use a Cholesky factor of Sigma instead. In that case you would put</li></ol><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">parameters </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  cholesky_factor_corr</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">J</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> L</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  vector</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\">lower</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">J</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sigma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>model </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  y </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> multi_normal_cholesky</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">zero</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> diag_pre_multiply</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">sigma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> L</span><span style=\"color:#660\">));</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><code><span style=\"color:#080\"><span style=\"color:#000\">sigma </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> gamma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></span></code><span style=\"color:#000\">  L </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> lkj_corr_cholesky</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">nu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>generated quantities </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  matrix</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">J</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">J</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Omega</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  matrix</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">J</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">J</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Sigma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Omega</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#000\"><code><span style=\"color:#000\">multiply_lower_tri_self_transpose</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">L</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;<br></span></code>  </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Sigma</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> quad_form_diag</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">sigma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">Omega);</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> <br></span><code><span style=\"color:#080\"></span></code><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div><br>It is always much better from a numerical / speed perspective to utilize Cholesky factors of symmetric positive definite matrices rather than the symmetric positive definite matrices themselves.<br><br>Ben<br><br></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "tsYlIO0PBQAJ",
   "from": "Stéphane Laurent",
   "author": "129e07bdb789652f",
   "date": "2016-03-06T18:53:44Z",
   "subject": "Re: LKJ prior - Sigma not positive definite or not symmetric",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Thank you ! I will try that. <div>As a side question, is there a difference between <b>generated quantities</b> and <b>transformed parameters</b> ?</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "1BzCRToTBQAJ",
   "from": "Ben Goodrich",
   "author": "743bbacc59f4b25f",
   "date": "2016-03-06T19:54:14Z",
   "subject": "Re: LKJ prior - Sigma not positive definite or not symmetric",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">On Sunday, March 6, 2016 at 1:53:44 PM UTC-5, Stéphane Laurent wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Thank you ! I will try that. <div>As a side question, is there a difference between <b>generated quantities</b> and <b>transformed parameters</b> ?</div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br>Yes, there is a large difference. The objects declared in the transformed parameters block are intended to be used in the likelihood of the data and hence are evaluated every time the log-posterior is evaluated, which can be thousands of times per iteration, and the autodiff mechanism has to be used in order to evaluate the derivative of the log-posterior with respect to each of the parameters declared in the parameters block. The objects declared in the generated quantities block are not intended to be used in the likelihood of the data, and hence are only evaluated once per iteration and do not require autodiff. In addition, objects declared in the transformed parameters block can be checked for validity (although this is really only good for catching programming mistakes) whereas the objects declared in the generated quantities block (and model block) are not validated, which saves time.<br><br>Ben<br><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "YWkSFC1HBQAJ",
   "from": "Stéphane Laurent",
   "author": "129e07bdb789652f",
   "date": "2016-03-07T11:46:12Z",
   "subject": "Re: LKJ prior - Sigma not positive definite or not symmetric",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Thank you again Ben, I see. <div><br></div><div>Just a correction here :</div><div><br></div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#606\">Sigma</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> quad_form_diag</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">sigma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Omega</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span></div></code></div><div><br></div><div>This should be :</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#606\">Sigma</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> quad_form_diag</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#606\">Omega</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sigma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span></div></code></div><br><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  }
 ]
}
